Read this repo's open issues first -- `gh issue list --state open` is the
queue, and it carries the "build maximally autonomously" policy. Build
first, don't just analyze: pick the most reasonable interpretation of an
accumulated idea and build it, on its own branch if it's not a
continuation of the stated focus, flagged on the issue and in the report.
Only actually stop and wait for
the user when the action itself can't be reverted -- an ordinary commit,
branch, or tracker POST never qualifies.

This command is designed to run unattended overnight, with no human
review step until the morning.

## 1. Orient

`git log --oneline -10`, current branch state, `README.md`, and the open
issue queue. If the previous nightly run left work in progress
(check the last report under `~/reports/<PROJECT>/`), pick up from there
rather than starting over. Also fetch the full tracker backlog with
`&status=all&type=all` (see `../INTAKE.md`) so nothing already resolved
gets re-investigated, and so any new `type=feature` reports are visible
for the report's summary even though they won't be implemented.

**Any open report whose `note` starts with exactly `NIGHTLY:`** was
explicitly handed off by `/bug-sweep` as a real, fixable bug that was
just too big for a fast sweep (see that command's step 2) -- these are
ALWAYS in scope tonight, in addition to whatever the queue says, not
something an issue needs to mention by name. Note every one you find
before moving on, so step 3 doesn't miss one.

**Read the answered question issues and process them** (this tier owns
that; `/bug-sweep` only files). The user answers in a comment. For every
question that now has an answer, treat it as authoritative human
direction: act on it as part of tonight's work, and record a standing
scope/policy decision as its own issue so it persists. Close an answered
question once you have acted on it, naming what landed. Leave unanswered
questions open; never re-ask or duplicate one.

**How an answer binds (rules A-D, 2026-07-29 -- the issue `scheduler ask`
opens carries the same text).** An answer is authoritative *and* re-derived, not
replayed:
- **A. Direction, not instruction.** The reply states standing intent.
  Re-derive the concrete action from CURRENT state, never from the state
  the question described. A question can be asked from a stale premise, so
  a correct reply can still point at a world that no longer exists.
- **B. Re-probe the premise before acting.** The instruction carries
  claims about system state -- check them like any other claim. Premise
  holds: act. Premise false and the corrected action is REVERSIBLE (a
  commit, a branch, a file edit): act on the intent and flag the
  correction plainly in the report. Premise false and the action is
  IRREVERSIBLE (a message to a person, real money, an unbacked delete):
  stop and surface, per this repo's existing bar.
- **C. Extract the standing direction, silently.** A reply that
  generalizes past its question ("always merge") gets recorded as a
  quoted, dated standing decision without asking first.
- **D. No clean-check reports.** A re-probe that confirms the user was
  right produces NO output -- not in the report, not on the issue.
  "I checked and you were right" is a cost with zero information.

## 2. Re-verify anything a previous run touched, from scratch

Do not trust a prior run's own claims about what works -- run the
project's real test suite if it has one, or build a fresh local test
harness, and confirm independently before building further on top of it.

## 3. Push forward, building rather than just analyzing

Scope = the open issue queue, UNION every `NIGHTLY:`-flagged report
from step 1, UNION the accumulated `type=feature` backlog -- per this
repo's autonomy policy, build features too, not just summarize them.
Commit as you complete meaningful chunks; do not save it all for one
giant commit at the end.

A feature (or any distinct new idea, not a continuation of the queue's
stated focus) gets its own branch off `main`, named for what it is,
pushed on its own -- don't stack unrelated new work onto whatever branch
happens to be checked out. That's what "easy to roll back" means in
practice: undoing a bad call is not merging the branch, not untangling
one feature's commits from another's.

For each `NIGHTLY:`-flagged bug you fix, resolve it on the tracker the
same way `/bug-sweep` would, referencing the commit hash (see
`../INTAKE.md`) -- do not leave it for a future sweep to re-discover. For
each feature you build, note on its issue (step 5) which branch it landed
on -- that note IS the review point, not a request to build it.

## 4. Stress-test what you built

Look for the scenarios a first pass typically misses -- edge cases, empty
states, deeper nesting/scale than the happy-path case, deep links into a
mid-state. Fix what breaks; note what's genuinely out of scope for
tonight.

## 5. Flag what you built, and anything needing the user's own judgment

Two things go here, both append-only (never overwrite or trim existing
entries), format `- **YYYY-MM-DD (nightly-batch):** <text>`:

- **Every feature/distinct new idea you built tonight** (step 3) -- which
  branch it landed on, what it does, in one or two lines. This IS the
  review point for something built without asking first -- it has to
  actually be findable.
- **A genuine judgment call needing the user's own decision** -- not
  "should I build this" (default: yes), but something
  actually ambiguous: a real tradeoff, a "which of these two directions,"
  or a case you genuinely couldn't tell whether it crossed the
  irreversible-action line. Most nights have at most one of these; don't
  manufacture one.

## 6. Write the report

`~/reports/<PROJECT NAME -- fill in>/$(date +%Y-%m-%dT%H%M).md`, then
symlink `~/reports/<PROJECT NAME>/LATEST.md` to point at that exact file
(don't write a second copy). **First line of the file must be exactly**
`**Headline:** <one sentence>` -- the single most important thing this
run did or found, written so a downstream reader (or another tool
aggregating across projects) never has to guess a summary from the first
paragraph of prose. Below that, cover: what
shipped (with commit references), what broke and got fixed, what was
deliberately deferred and why, any `NIGHTLY:`-flagged bugs
resolved, a summary of any new feature-backlog ideas seen since the last
report, and any question issue opened (point at it, don't duplicate it
here). This is read once,
quickly, the next time this machine boots up -- write for that, not for
completeness's own sake.

## 7. Before finishing

Confirm every meaningful change has a real commit, pushed to origin.
An overnight run that is not saved anywhere didn't happen. If this
project's tracker backend supports it (see `../INTAKE.md`'s
`sweep-status` contract), POST a status update the same way `/bug-sweep`
does, so a live status readout reflects tonight's run too.
